<http://data.silknow.org/object/ffdab11f-a283-365a-9be8-06d2595bfed8>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Huizong was the eighth emperor of the Song dynasty and the most artistically accomplished of his imperial line. Finches and Bamboo exemplifies the realistic style of flower-and-­bird painting practiced at Huizong’s academy. Whether making a study from nature or illustrating a line of poetry, however, the emperor valued capturing the spirit of a subject over literal representation. Here the minutely observed finches are imbued with the vitality of their living counterparts. Drops of lacquer added to the birds’eyes impart a final lifelike touch."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/587d7273-58e9-3d8b-8bfc-5ff4d744538a> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/2cae0128-a5cb-3858-8bde-c8b36c236909> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/09b681f6-d511-3ce7-bf46-00fb94b11474> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7c11ffdf-e38b-34d3-b912-7df254df5c08> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/0ce1fd31-85a3-3da7-82c0-6ce22214accf> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b83e6728-d54f-3cf6-9979-629e1a21ec07> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b35e5928-e484-32bb-a6e7-cd256983adf2> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/a347796d-5448-378c-a0e9-f6f7c880e406> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/051b4149-f492-32ba-9be6-13e3b49aa80b> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b5e3d06a-cf60-3b93-8456-064a4ba57f55> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/6efad43c-64e9-30d6-950a-0e10647782a7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/3cddab3f-4355-31c7-b34d-89782597defc> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/593b5bfe-6d0e-3ac2-8a54-20071b96d7e7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/c5fbc6c9-2a09-31e8-a0ef-1dba699acfb1> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/ebcb22dd-1256-3876-b0f4-9bb21205c791> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/5ae40919-b6a4-38f7-b6f8-a95ce965dc4c> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/830a24f7-2456-3cd8-8909-66da281d38b6> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Huizong was the eighth emperor of the Song dynasty and the most artistically accomplished of his imperial line. Finches and Bamboo exemplifies the realistic style of flower-and-­bird painting practiced at Huizong’s academy. Whether making a study from nature or illustrating a line of poetry, however, the emperor valued capturing the spirit of a subject over literal representation. Here the minutely observed finches are imbued with the vitality of their living counterparts. Drops of lacquer added to the birds’eyes impart a final lifelike touch."@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "1981.278" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
